{"name":"Golden Field","hex":"#c39e44","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"golden","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of, or relating to, gold.","Having a color or other richness suggestive of gold."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["(Can we verify this sense?) brassy drummer, brassy chub, etc. (Kyphosus vaigiensis), a fish found in southeast Asia.","Ellipsis of golden retriever."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/golden"},{"term":"field","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A land area free of woodland, cities, and towns; an area of open country.","The open country near or belonging to a town or city."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To intercept or catch (a ball) and play it.","To be the team catching and throwing the ball, as opposed to hitting it."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/field"}],"entries":[{"title":"Golden field","blocks":[{"text":"In mathematics, ⁠⁠, sometimes called the golden field, is a number system consisting of the set of all numbers ⁠⁠, where ⁠⁠ and ⁠⁠ are both rational numbers and ⁠⁠ is the square root of 5, along with the basic arithmetical operations. This arithmetic shares certain structural properties with the arithmetic of ⁠⁠, the field of rational numbers, making ⁠⁠ also a type of field. More specifically, it is a real quadratic field, the extension field of ⁠⁠ generated by combining rational numbers and ⁠⁠ using arithmetical operations. The name comes from the golden ratio ⁠⁠, a positive number satisfying the equation ⁠⁠, which is the fundamental unit of ⁠⁠."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golden_field","wikidata":"Q135087801"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://